35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"I fear we are losing the hands that do the work. Many skilled technicians have departed, leaving a void that no amount of funding can fill instantly.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"I found something weird! The money for travel is stuck, but the agency is still paying for the glasshouses! Some projects might be too expensive if they don't use those subsidies.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that the ""flashy"" projects often just stop. The ones that were simpler, more focused on data and utility, actually finished and are still useful today.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"I knew it. The equipment is breaking and the service contracts are a mess. If a project depends on a single machine that's always broken, it's a waste of money.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The situation is mixed. Computing is accessible and affordable, but the glasshouses are completely full. Any project requiring plant space is currently impossible.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Minako, that is a critical point. If the glasshouses are full and the technicians are gone, a biological project becomes a dream, not a research plan.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"Exactly! If a project needs a lot of travel or special tools that aren't subsidized, they'll run out of money super fast! We should pick something ""lean.""","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,I agree with Sakura... I think. The data shows that projects relying on computation are more likely to actually deliver their results on time.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"I'm not just talking about money. I'm talking about time. If the project relies on a breeding colony or a fragile machine, it's going to get interrupted. I won't vote for that.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minako,,"We must weigh the biological risks against the computational ones. While computing is open, we must ensure the project doesn't just produce ""noise"" without physical validation.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,We must be honest with ourselves. We cannot award a grant to a project that requires expertise we no longer possess in our halls.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"Let's look for the project that fits the ""free"" stuff! If the agency pays for the glasshouse, but the project doesn't need a million technicians, that's the winner!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"But... if the glasshouses are full, as Minako said... then maybe the computational projects are the only ones that won't just... stall.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"Right. I'm against anything with high environmental or equipment-failure risks. If it needs a specific machine that's currently a disaster, it's a no from me.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minako,,The bottleneck is clear: physical space and specialized staff. A project that leverages the current computing agreement while requiring minimal facility footprints is the most viable.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
