60,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Candidate D requires greenhouse space and technicians. Given the staffing shortages and full facilities, I cannot in good conscience support it.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Sakura,,"D is out! Minako said the glasshouses are full! And C probably needs a lot of specialized care for the salamanders, which costs a lot of money!","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Candidate A is the safest. It's like the projects I saw in the closeout files. It's a catalog, it's useful, and it's less likely to just... stop.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Takumi,,Candidate B is a trap. It needs spectroscopy. I saw the logs; the spectroscopy suite is a disaster and the service contract is gone. B will just sit idle for months.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minako,,I agree. Candidate D is impossible due to glasshouse bookings. Candidate B is risky due to equipment instability. Candidate A is the most operationally sound.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Servas,,"Candidate A relies on sequencing and metadata. While it is less ""daring"" than B or C, it is a project that can actually be completed with the hands we have.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Sakura,,"A is great! It uses the national repository and computing, which are the things that are actually working and affordable right now. It's a smart choice!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I'm worried that some people might think A is too plain... but the history shows that ""plain"" projects are the ones that actually help other scientists for years.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"I don't care if it's ""plain."" I care if it's possible. A doesn't depend on a single broken machine or a full greenhouse. It's the only one that won't be interrupted.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minako,,"Candidate C also requires significant animal facility capacity. While the vivarium is accredited, the cost of day rates has risen sharply, making C a financial risk.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Servas,,It is better to build a sturdy bridge that people can cross than a golden bridge that collapses under its own weight. Candidate A is that sturdy bridge.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Sakura,,I'm voting for A! It's the only one that doesn't look like it'll run out of money or get stuck in a waiting list for a room!,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,I... I feel more confident now. I think A is the right choice because it provides lasting value without the risks that usually cause projects to fail.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"Finally, we're thinking logically. No fragile equipment, no full greenhouses, no staffing nightmares. A is the only one that makes sense.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Minako,,The evidence is consistent. Candidate A aligns with our current infrastructure capacity and budget constraints. It is the most realistic choice for a national grant.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
